.TH OTPME-ACLGROUP 1 "2026-10-30" "OTPme 0.3.0a283" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-aclgroup \- manage OTPme ACL groups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-aclgroup
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIaclgroup\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-aclgroup
manages ACL groups.
An ACL group is a set of ACLs.
You give the group on an object instead of giving the ACLs one by one.
.PP
Each entry of a group names an object type and an ACL, for example \fBuser:edit:attribute\fR or \fBtoken:view:initial_secret\fR.
On an object the group grants the entries for the type of that object, and nothing else.
On a user it grants the \fBuser:\fR entries, on a token the \fBtoken:\fR entries.
.PP
A group is given with \fBadd_acl\fR of the object, as \fB@\fIgroup\fR, in place of an ACL:
.RS
.TP
.B @\fIgroup\fR
On the object itself.
.TP
.B +@\fIgroup\fR
On the members of the unit, or on the tokens of the user.
.TP
.B ++@\fIgroup\fR
On the whole tree below the unit: its sub units, their members, and the tokens of the users in it.
.RE
.PP
\fB+@\fIgroup\fR and \fB++@\fIgroup\fR do not count on the unit or user itself.
Give \fB@\fIgroup\fR as well for that, for example \fB@usermanager,++@usermanager\fR.
.PP
A group of another site is written as \fB@\fIsite\fB/\fIgroup\fR.
.PP
Change a group, and the change counts at once on every object the group is given on.
Nothing has to be written to those objects again.
.PP
A group can be a member of another group, the same way as a role in a role.
The member then inherits the entries of the other group, and of every group above it.
A member that would inherit from itself is refused.
.PP
A disabled group grants nothing, and passes nothing on to its members.
.SS Base Groups
Every site has these groups:
.TP
.B realm_user
What every user of the realm may see.
The role \fBREALM_USER\fR has it on the site and on the whole tree below it.
It holds \fBview_public\fR on the site, units, users, groups, roles, hosts and nodes, and \fBview:attribute\fR on the site, units, users and groups.
That is what an LDAP client and \fBotpme\-shell\fR need.
Access groups, clients, policies and the other object types are not in it: a user does not see them.
.TP
.B usermanager
Add, change and delete users in a unit, add tokens to them, and see the secret and the PIN of a new token (\fBview:initial_secret\fR, \fBview:initial_pin\fR, see
.BR otpme\-token (1)).
No temporary passwords.
.TP
.B useradmin
Everything of \fBusermanager\fR, and more: move and rename users and tokens, keys, policies, temporary passwords, and the other token ACLs.
.TP
.B groupmanager
Add and delete groups in a unit, and add and remove their members.
.PP
\fBget_acls\fR shows the exact entries.
OTPme sets them, and an update of OTPme may add new ones.
You cannot change their entries, rename them or delete them.
You can make your own groups members of them, to inherit their entries and add your own.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS ACL Group Management
.TP
.B add \fIaclgroup\fR
Create a new ACL group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:aclgroup\fR on the unit.
.TP
.B del \fIaclgroup\fR
Delete an ACL group.
It says how many members it has, and on how many objects it is given.
The ACLs that name it stay on those objects, as orphans; \fBremove_orphans\fR of an object takes them away.
.TP
.B show \fR[\fIaclgroup\fR]
Show ACL groups, with their entries and members.
.na
.nh
.TP
.B show_oid \fR[\fB\-\-full\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR
Show the OID of the ACL group: \fB\%aclgroup|<realm>/<site>/<name>\fR.
With \fB\-\-full\fR the full OID, which names the unit as well: \fB\%aclgroup|<realm>/<site>/<unit_path>/<name>\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.TP
.B show_uuid \fIaclgroup\fR
Show the UUID of the ACL group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.hy
.ad
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIregex\fR]
List ACL groups, optionally filtered by regex pattern.
.TP
.B enable \fIaclgroup\fR
Enable a disabled ACL group.
.TP
.B disable \fIaclgroup\fR
Disable an ACL group without deleting it.
It grants nothing until it is enabled again.
.TP
.B rename \fIaclgroup\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename an ACL group.
The objects it is given on keep it: they name it by UUID.
.TP
.B move \fR[\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR \fIunit\fR
Move the ACL group to a different unit.
.TP
.B touch \fIaclgroup\fR
Re-index the object to fix potential index problems.
.TP
.B auto_disable \fIaclgroup\fR \fItime\fR
Set auto\-disable time, for example to let a delegation end by itself.
It is either a relative time with a leading \fB+\fR, e.g. \fB+30D\fR, or an absolute one, e.g. \fB\(dq09:53 13.06.2023\(dq\fR.
The units are \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBD\fR, \fBW\fR, \fBM\fR and \fBY\fR, see
.BR otpme (7).
\fB0\fR removes it.
.SS Entries
.TP
.B add_entry \fIaclgroup\fR \fIobject_type\fB:\fIacl\fR[\fB,\fIobject_type\fB:\fIacl\fR]
Add entries to the ACL group.
The ACL must be one the object type has; \fB_show_supported_acls\fR of an object of that type lists them.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:entry\fR.
.TP
.B remove_entry \fIaclgroup\fR \fIobject_type\fB:\fIacl\fR[\fB,\fIobject_type\fB:\fIacl\fR]
Remove entries from the ACL group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:entry\fR.
.TP
.B get_acls \fR[\fB\-r\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR
Show the entries of the ACL group.
With \fB\-r\fR also the entries it inherits, each with the group it comes from.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:entries\fR.
.SS Members
.TP
.B add_aclgroup \fIaclgroup\fR \fImember\fR
Make \fImember\fR a member of the ACL group.
\fImember\fR then inherits the entries of the ACL group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:aclgroup\fR on the ACL group.
.TP
.B remove_aclgroup \fIaclgroup\fR \fImember\fR
Take \fImember\fR out of the ACL group.
A UUID is taken as well, which is how a member that is gone is cleared out.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:aclgroup\fR.
.TP
.B list_aclgroups \fR[\fB\-r\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR
List the members of the ACL group.
With \fB\-r\fR also their members, and so on.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:aclgroups\fR.
.SS Object Changelog
Every change to the object writes an entry: who made it, when, and what was done.
A change can carry a comment of its own, through the global option \fB\-\-changelog\fR.
.PP
An \fIentry_id\fR is the number the listing shows in front of an entry, counted from 0.
.PP
Only the comment of an entry can be changed or removed.
What was done and by whom stays as it is.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B changelog \fIaclgroup\fR
Show the changelog of the ACL group.
Needs the ACL \fBview:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fIaclgroup\fR \fIentry_id\fR \fIcomment\fR
Set the comment of that entry, or replace the one it has.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fIaclgroup\fR \fIentry_id\fR
Remove the comment of that entry.
The entry itself stays, and an entry without a comment is refused.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fIaclgroup\fR
Remove every entry of the changelog.
.br
This needs more than the ACL \fBclear:changelog\fR: the config parameter
.B allow_changelog_clearing
has to allow it (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
.SS Policy Management
A \fIpolicy\fR is the name of a policy, not a path.
It is looked for in the site of the ACL group.
Which policies there are, and what each of them does, is in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.TP
.B add_policy \fIaclgroup\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Attach a policy to the ACL group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBassign:policy\fR.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fIaclgroup\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Remove a policy from the ACL group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:policy\fR.
.TP
.B list_policies \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR
List the policies attached to the ACL group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:policies\fR.
.SS ACL Management
These are the ACLs on the ACL group itself: who may change it.
.TP
.B add_acl \fIaclgroup\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR
Add an access control entry.
.TP
.B del_acl \fIaclgroup\fR \fIacl\fR
Remove an access control entry.
.TP
.B list_acls \fIaclgroup\fR
Show the ACLs on the ACL group.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fIaclgroup\fR
Let a move of the ACL group swap its default ACLs.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBenable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fIaclgroup\fR
A move leaves the ACLs of the ACL group as they are again.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdisable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B config \fR[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-d\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set, append to or delete a config parameter.
.TP
.B get_config \fIaclgroup\fR \fIparameter\fR
Show a config parameter.
.TP
.B description \fIaclgroup\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set the description of the ACL group.
.TP
.B info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set free-form info text. If \fIinfo\fR is omitted, the current info text is
opened in the editor specified by the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable.
.TP
.B dump_info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR
Dump the info text to stdout.
.SS Import/Export
.TP
.B export \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIPASS\fR] \fIaclgroup\fR
Export the configuration of the ACL group.
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fIaclgroup\fR
Remove members that are gone.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Display Options
.TP
.B \-a
Show all ACL groups.
.TP
.B \-z \fISIZE\fR
Limit output size.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIFIELD1,FIELD2\fR
Display only specified fields.
.TP
.B \-\-policy\-limit \fIN\fR
Limit number of policies shown.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
Limit number of items shown per object.
.TP
.B \-\-sort\-by \fIFIELD\fR
Sort output by field.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse sort order.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Output without headers/borders.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Output as CSV.
.TP
.B \-\-csv\-sep \fISEP\fR
CSV separator character.
.SS General Options
.TP
.B \-r
Recursive, for \fBget_acls\fR and \fBlist_aclgroups\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-acls
Preserve ACLs when moving the ACL group.
.TP
.B \-\-password \fIPASS\fR
Password for encrypting exports.
.PP
Global options are available for all commands. See
.BR otpme (1)
for details.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B otpme-aclgroup get_acls usermanager
Show what the base group \fBusermanager\fR holds.
.TP
.B otpme-unit add_acl linux/users role helpdesk @usermanager,++@usermanager
Let the role \fBhelpdesk\fR manage the users of the unit \fBlinux/users\fR and its sub units, and their tokens.
\fB@usermanager\fR lets it add users to \fBlinux/users\fR itself, \fB++@usermanager\fR counts below it.
.TP
.B otpme-aclgroup add it-user-manager
Create an ACL group of your own.
.TP
.B otpme-aclgroup add_aclgroup usermanager it-user-manager
Let \fBit-user-manager\fR inherit everything of \fBusermanager\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-aclgroup add_entry it-user-manager user:set_temp_password,token:set_temp_password
Add what \fBusermanager\fR does not have.
.TP
.B otpme-aclgroup get_acls \-r it-user-manager
Show its entries and the ones it inherits.
.TP
.B otpme-unit add_acl linux/users role helpdesk @it-user-manager,++@it-user-manager
Give it on the unit.
.TP
.B otpme-aclgroup auto_disable it-user-manager +30D
End the delegation in 30 days.
